for years  they've been called illegal now the  government says  they are essential that's the confusing  reality for  at least half of the 2.4 million farm  workers  who plant and harvest our food here in  the united states  many produce pickers are working under  grueling conditions  with few safety measures in place and  that was already a problem  even before hundreds began testing  positive for coronavirus  in farms across the country  [Music]  after 13 years working in the fields of  southern california  carmelita still does not have legal  status in the u.s  and because of that she cannot even  think about signing up for basic  benefits  like health insurance and she doesn't  qualify for a stimulus  check from the federal government  overcoming these kinds of challenges  isn't anything new for carmelita  what's different is that now she's  terrified of getting sick  by mid-may a vast majority of farm  laborers around the country  say they had still not received masks  gloves or any other protective equipment  from the companies they work for  carmelita had been taking her  own precautions but many of her  co-workers  didn't believe or understand the  outbreak so keeping social distance has  been difficult  with peak harvesting season already  underway farmworker advocates fear  that negligence and lack of education  could lead to a major outbreak  that in turn could further disrupt an  already fragile supply chain  and leave empty shelves in grocery  stores  we're treated as essential workers right  now  because if we don't do this kind of work  the united states is not going to have  food in  in supermarkets food to to feed  the nation one doctor from the same  county carmelita lives in  has been trying to help us  has been going from farm to farm  educating workers about the virus  they live in our community they function  in our society so we want to get  into them and really help them be safe  just trying to provide basic medical  education  on how to keep the infrastructure of our  society safe to keep the rest  not all farms have welcomed him but he  says  some are taking steps to protect their  workers  good farms which hires domestic workers  and international workers with temporary  work permits  is putting hand wash stations in the  fields  part of the work that we're having to do  is  to not only look at the work but also  interface with our teams in both their  personal lives  and the lives of their families  california leads the nation in growing  produce  and is home to 400 000 farm workers  of which an estimated 50 percent are  unauthorized  in mid-april california governor gavin  newsom announced  a one-time covet 19 relief payment of  500  per adult to approximately 150  000 unauthorized immigrants he also said  that farms and other food industry  businesses  must give workers an additional two  weeks of covert 19 sick leave  that would make a huge difference for  carmelita who says her company  normally only gives three sick days per  year  more sick leave right now would help  with the current situation  but this kind of work can be  backbreaking at the best of times  see advocates and the challenges for  america's undocumented farm workers  and ultimately its food supply remain  when carmelita finishes her 12 hour  shifts picking strawberries  she heads to her two children at a  rented room  in a mobile home  she dreams of the security that legal  status would bring america  but the options for applying are limited  and it can be very expensive  the pandemic has only made it harder  president donald trump has used the  outbreak to suspend  most legal immigration temporary guest  worker programs have continued to  protect the food supply  but those programs only cover 10 percent  of farm workers in the u.s  and it doesn't help carmelita since  she's been here without legal status  for more than a decade  immigrants  the blue card is part of a law proposed  by democrats in 2017  that would give agricultural workers the  right to work  and a path to permanent legal status for  them  and their families  sons were born in the u.s making them  american citizens  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  a  [Music]  you

